WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.382
Project A uses subqueries to build a patient-level feature table.

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:10.957
Each patient receives an admission count, abnormal-lab count, and distinct-medication count.

00:00:10.957 --> 00:00:15.332
The query then combines those features into a simple teaching score.

00:00:15.332 --> 00:00:17.917
The score is not a clinical model.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:24.857
The SQL lesson is to create features at one clear grain and then calculate a derived value from

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:30.662
them. Connect this idea to an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product.

00:00:30.662 --> 00:00:36.372
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.372 --> 00:00:44.182
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:44.182 --> 00:00:49.977
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:49.977 --> 00:00:56.113
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
